insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-water-arrives', 1, 'Water influx We is the one term in the material balance that has to be modelled rather than measured. Why?', '["It crosses a subsurface boundary that carries no meter, unlike production and pressure, which are both instrumented", "It is too small to resolve against the combined uncertainty of the oil, gas and water production allocations", "It is measured accurately at the oil water contact but only after the field has produced its first water", "It can be read directly from the cumulative produced water column once the water cut has stabilised above ten percent"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Np, Gp, Wp, pressure and the formation volume factors all come from instruments or a laboratory. Water crossing the oil water contact does not, so an aquifer model has to generate it. Produced water is a different quantity: influx often runs for years before any water is produced.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-water-arrives', 2, 'Dividing the balance through by total expansion gives F/Et = N + We/Et. What does that guarantee about the apparent oil in place when influx is ignored?', '["It is too high early in the history and then swings too low once cumulative influx overtakes the total expansion term", "It is unbiased on average across a long history, because the contamination term changes sign as the drawdown keeps growing", "It is above the true oil in place at every survey, because We and Et are both positive once production starts", "It is too low, because the imported water occupies pore volume that the oil would otherwise have filled"]'::jsonb, 2, 'We is positive whenever water is arriving and Et is positive whenever pressure has fallen, so the contamination term is positive at every survey. Ignored influx always flatters the booking: water does work the model attributes to oil, so the model needs more oil to do it.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-water-arrives', 3, 'A constructed tank of 12000000 stb was run down the same pressure path twice, once closed and once with a 20000000 rb pot aquifer. The closed version produced 259497.841573946 stb by the last survey and the aquifer version 386601.813549014 stb. What does that comparison show?', '["That the aquifer raised the recovery factor by lowering the residual oil saturation left behind the advancing water front", "That water support buys more oil for the same pressure budget, here 48.9807434251238 percent more at the same final pressure", "That the aquifer version must have depleted faster, since it reached exactly the same final pressure while producing more oil than its twin", "That the two tanks cannot be compared, because the aquifer version has a different oil in place"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both tanks hold the same 12000000 stb and follow the same pressure decline. The extra 127103.971975068 stb is the water that arrived doing work the oil would otherwise have had to do, so the pressure budget bought more oil. Nothing about sweep or residual saturation is in the tank model.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-water-arrives', 4, 'The six surveys of that aquifer tank were handed to a closed tank model. The fit returned 17874138.5678105 stb with an R-squared of 0.999999892672973 and an intercept of 151.296075046121 rb. What is the correct reading?', '["The fit is sound, since an R-squared above 0.9999 with a near zero intercept rules out a missing term", "The intercept of 151.296075046121 rb is the influx, and adding it back recovers the true oil in place", "The R-squared is meaningless because six points is below the minimum sample size for a regression", "The answer is 48.9511547317538 percent too high, and no straightness statistic could have detected it"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The truth built into that tank is 12000000 stb. A fit statistic tests whether points lie on a line, and these do, because the influx is proportional to the drawdown and rescales the relationship without bending it. Straightness cannot see a missing term of that shape.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-water-arrives', 5, 'Reading a column of F/Et values survey by survey, which two features are independent of one another and must be judged separately?', '["The scatter of the points about the fitted line and the number of surveys contributing to the fit", "The sign of the intercept and the magnitude of the slope reported by the least squares regression", "The level at which the column sits and the drift, meaning whether the column moves as surveys go on", "The pressure range covered by the surveys and the cumulative oil produced across that range"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The column equals N plus We/Et. Where it sits reflects how much contamination is present, and whether it moves reflects how the contamination changes with time. A flat column says only that the contamination is not changing, which is much weaker than saying there is none.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-water-arrives', 6, 'A constructed tank with a 5000000 rb pot aquifer, true oil in place 12000000 stb, gave a ratio column that moved only -0.0375706439360688 percent across six surveys while sitting about 12.24 percent above the truth. What does that case establish?', '["That an aquifer delivering in step with the drawdown is invisible to the drift test and shifts only the level", "That the ratio column drifts downward whenever an aquifer is present and upward when the tank is closed", "That a 5000000 rb aquifer is too small to affect the material balance of a twelve million barrel tank", "That the surveys must have been recorded out of order, since a genuine influx always produces a rising column"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Pot influx is proportional to drawdown and total expansion is very nearly proportional to drawdown, so their ratio barely changes. The column stays flat, the line stays straight, and the model absorbs the imported volume by inflating the oil in place instead.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-water-arrives', 7, 'Given that a flat ratio column cannot detect an aquifer whose influx tracks the pressure drop, which instrument can see that the level is wrong?', '["The R-squared of the straight line, once it is quoted to enough decimal places to resolve the curvature", "The reconciliation against an independent volumetric booking of the same field", "The residuals of the fit, which turn systematically positive at the outer end of the plot when water is arriving", "The cumulative produced water column, which begins to rise as soon as the aquifer starts delivering volume"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The dynamic diagnostics all test internal consistency, which a proportional influx does not disturb. Only an estimate produced by a different route, from a map and a contact rather than from pressure and produced volumes, can say that the level itself is too high.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-water-arrives', 8, 'Why does a ratio column drift upward when the aquifer lags rather than delivering instantly?', '["Because produced water is subtracted from the underground withdrawal, which shrinks the numerator as the field''s water cut climbs", "Because the oil formation volume factor keeps rising with depletion, inflating the withdrawal faster than the expansion terms grow", "Because cumulative influx keeps accumulating with elapsed time while Et is tied to a drawdown that flattens as the field ages", "Because rock compaction accelerates once the effective stress exceeds the in situ horizontal stress"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A lagging aquifer delivers volume that depends on how long the drawdown has been applied as well as how large it is, so We keeps growing even while pressure barely moves. A quantity that keeps accumulating divided by one that is flattening gives a rising ratio.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-water-arrives', 9, 'On a tank whose model is missing a water influx term, which direction does the drift in the ratio column run?', '["Downward, since the imported water raises the pressure and therefore reduces the computed total expansion", "Either direction, depending on whether the aquifer is above or below the reservoir in the structure", "Downward at first and then upward, once cumulative influx overtakes cumulative oil expansion", "Upward, always, because the contamination term is positive and grows relative to the expansion"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The apparent value is the true oil in place plus We/Et, and a lagging influx makes that term grow. There is no mechanism in the tank model that would make it shrink under falling pressure, so a falling column points at a bookkeeping problem rather than at an aquifer.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-water-arrives', 10, 'A weak lagging aquifer on a 12000000 stb constructed tank gave apparent values from 12191683.0179475 stb at the first survey to 13238914.6524684 stb at the sixth. How should that be reported?', '["As a drift, spanning 8.58972164039356 percent, with the last survey 10.3242887705698 percent above the truth", "As scatter of about ten percent, which is within normal survey to survey measurement uncertainty", "As an oil in place of about 12.7 million stb, taking the average of the six apparent values", "As a compartmentalisation signal, since the apparent volume grows as new parts of the tank are drained"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The movement is monotonic and its increments grow every step, which is the accelerating shape of influx rather than scatter or a data event. Averaging a drifting column produces a number belonging to no survey and no reservoir.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-water-arrives', 11, 'On that same weak drifting tank the closed model returned an R-squared of 0.998861013127565, while fitting the first three surveys and the last three separately gave slopes differing by 15.5366204795710 percent. What follows?', '["That the last three surveys are unreliable and should be excluded from the fit as outliers", "That the subset test detects the missing term at a drift where the fit statistic still looks excellent", "That the regression should be forced through the origin, which will bring the two subset slopes together", "That the pressure gauge was recalibrated partway through the history, producing a step in the survey record"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Gently curving points still correlate well, so the fit statistic stays high. Splitting the history and fitting each half separately compares the tank against itself and exposes a disagreement no goodness of fit measure can show.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-water-arrives', 12, 'Two lagging aquifer tanks returned closed model intercepts of -8741.16069060488 rb and -43705.8034530246 rb. What does the sign of those intercepts tell you?', '["That the cumulative production was entered as period volumes rather than as running totals", "That water was leaving the reservoir for the aquifer, since a negative fitted intercept can only mean a negative cumulative influx", "That the influx must have begun well before the first survey was taken, which shifts the whole fitted line bodily downward", "Very little about the mechanism, because the sign follows the curvature of the data rather than the missing term"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Drifting data curves upward, and a least squares line through upward curving points is steeper than the ray from the origin through their middle, so it crosses the withdrawal axis below zero. Treat a non zero intercept as a flag, and read the direction from the ratio column instead.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-water-arrives', 13, 'Which set of aquifer models does the material balance engine offer?', '["none, pot, fetkovich and carter_tracy", "pot, fetkovich, carter_tracy and van_everdingen_hurst, with none expressed by setting the aquifer size to zero", "pot, schilthuis steady state, fetkovich and carter_tracy, selected by the solver method rather than a model field", "none, pot and fetkovich, with Carter-Tracy reached through the history matching parameters instead"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine''s aquifer model type is a union of exactly those four values. Choosing none is a modelling assertion in its own right, not the absence of a model: it claims the reservoir has no aquifer in effective pressure communication over the surveyed period.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-water-arrives', 14, 'A history has a missing observation_date on one row. Which models will still run, and why?', '["All four, because the engine falls back to evenly spaced timesteps when a date cannot be parsed", "Only Carter-Tracy, whose superposition is formulated in dimensionless time rather than in days", "The no aquifer and pot paths, because neither needs elapsed time, while Fetkovich and Carter-Tracy throw a named error", "Only the no aquifer path, since every aquifer model needs a time step to march its influx forward"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The pot model''s influx depends only on the current pressure, so it never asks for time deltas. The two time marching models do, and the engine refuses to guess: it stops, names the offending timestep, and suggests either adding the dates or switching to the time independent model.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-water-arrives', 15, 'How do the three aquifer models compare in what they demand from you?', '["All three need the same two inputs, aquifer size and productivity index, and differ only in how they march them forward", "Pot needs one size parameter, Fetkovich adds a productivity index and dated surveys, and Carter-Tracy is parameterised by geometry and rock properties", "Pot needs the full aquifer geometry, Fetkovich needs only the survey dates, and Carter-Tracy needs a measured pressure history inside the aquifer itself", "Pot and Fetkovich both need aquifer permeability, while Carter-Tracy replaces it with a lumped productivity index"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Appetite for information rises across the list, and so does the freedom each model hands to the fit. Choose the least elaborate model the diagnostic demands, say which one you chose and why, and name the observation that would change your mind.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-pot-aquifer', 1, 'What is the pot aquifer''s influx equation?', '["We equals the aquifer productivity index multiplied by the pressure difference and by the elapsed time since the previous survey", "We = (cw + cf) W (pi - p), with W the aquifer water in place in reservoir barrels and pi - p the drawdown from initial", "We equals the aquifer water in place multiplied by the ratio of current pressure to initial pressure, less one", "We equals Bti times the compressibility group divided by one minus the initial water saturation, all times the drawdown"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The aquifer is treated as a second tank of water at the reservoir''s pressure. Its water expands and its pore space compacts as pressure falls, and the freed volume crosses the boundary. Nothing about time, rate, permeability or distance appears anywhere in it.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-pot-aquifer', 2, 'The rock and connate water term uses the group (Swi cw + cf) while the aquifer influx term uses (cw + cf). Why do they differ?', '["Because the aquifer group has to be scaled up by one over one minus the initial water saturation before it is applied", "Because aquifer water is typically more saline than connate water and therefore carries a different compressibility", "Because formation compressibility applies to the reservoir rock only, and the aquifer contributes water expansion alone", "Because the aquifer''s pore space is entirely water, so the water saturation weighting on cw becomes one and disappears"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In the reservoir only the fraction Swi of the pore volume is water, so cw enters weighted. In the aquifer the saturation is one, so the weight vanishes. Formation compressibility is unweighted in both, because rock compacts regardless of what sits in the pores.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-pot-aquifer', 3, 'A pot aquifer of 20000000 rb delivered 154558.843226620 rb by the time the drawdown reached 1103.99173733300 psi, which is 0.772794216133100 percent of itself. What controls that fraction?', '["Only the compressibility group and the drawdown, so the same fraction applies whatever size the aquifer turns out to be", "The ratio of the aquifer volume to the reservoir pore volume, which sets how much of the water leg is in communication", "The aquifer permeability and the elapsed time, since a tighter water leg gives up its volume more slowly than a permeable one", "The initial water saturation of the reservoir, which determines how much of the freed volume can enter the oil column"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The influx is the compressibility group multiplied by the aquifer size and the drawdown, so dividing by the size leaves only the group and the drawdown. Water and rock are stiff, so even 1104 psi releases under one percent, which is why an effective aquifer has to be very large.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-pot-aquifer', 4, 'Which pair of axes does the engine use for its oil pot aquifer regression?', '["Underground withdrawal against total expansion, exactly as in the closed tank case, with the aquifer term added afterwards", "Underground withdrawal against the drawdown, so that the slope carries the tank stiffness in reservoir barrels per psi", "F/Em on the vertical axis against (pi - p)/Em on the horizontal, where Em is the fluid expansion Eo plus m times Eg", "F/Et on the vertical axis against the cumulative produced water on the horizontal, both normalised to initial conditions"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Grouping the drawdown proportional terms on the right and dividing through by the fluid expansion Em produces a straight line in those coordinates. On an oil tank with no gas cap m is zero, so Em is just the oil expansion.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-pot-aquifer', 5, 'In the pot aquifer regression, where is the oil in place read?', '["From the slope, as in the closed tank case, since the oil in place still multiplies the expansion term", "From the intercept, because the constant term of the rearranged balance is the oil in place standing alone", "From the ratio of the slope to the intercept, once the aquifer contribution has been subtracted from both", "From the point where the fitted line crosses the horizontal axis, extrapolated back to zero drawdown"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Dividing the balance by Em leaves N standing alone as the constant, with every drawdown proportional term collected into the slope. The Associate tier flagged that this would happen: adding a term moves the unknown you want from the slope to the intercept.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-pot-aquifer', 6, 'What does the slope of the pot aquifer plot contain?', '["Both the reservoir''s own rock and connate water term and the aquifer term (cw + cf) W, which are simply added together", "Only the aquifer term (cw + cf) W, since the rock and connate water expansion has already been removed by the denominator", "Only the reservoir''s rock and connate water response, with the aquifer contribution appearing in the intercept alongside the oil", "The product of the oil in place and the aquifer water in place, divided by the sum of the two compressibilities"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both quantities are proportional to drawdown, so both land in the slope when the balance is grouped that way. That is why the aquifer size cannot be read off the slope directly: the reservoir''s own contribution has to be subtracted first.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-pot-aquifer', 7, 'Why does the engine divide by Em rather than by the total expansion Et in its pot regression?', '["Because Et is undefined at the initial timestep, where the drawdown and every expansion term are exactly zero", "Because Et is quoted per stock tank barrel of original oil while Em is quoted per reservoir barrel of pore volume", "Because dividing by Et would make the plot dimensionless and remove the units that identify the slope as a volume", "Because Et already absorbs Efw into the denominator, which distorts the line and fails the published pot benchmark"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Efw is proportional to drawdown and its contribution scales with the oil in place, so it belongs in the slope alongside the aquifer term. Moving it into the horizontal axis mixes a quantity carrying the unknown N into the axis and the intercept stops being a clean N.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-pot-aquifer', 8, 'A pot regression on a constructed tank returned an intercept of 11999999.9999999 stb and a slope of 251.876923076925. The reservoir''s own contribution to the slope is 111.876923076923 rb/psi and the compressibility group is 0.000007 per psi. What is the aquifer water in place?', '["35982417.5824179 rb, from dividing the whole reported slope by the compressibility group with no subtraction", "20000000.0000003 rb, after removing the reservoir term from the slope", "15982417.5824176 rb, from dividing the reservoir''s own contribution to the slope by the compressibility group", "It cannot be recovered from a single regression, because the slope and the intercept share the same two unknowns"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Order of operations matters here. The intercept gives the oil in place, the oil in place gives the reservoir''s share of the slope, and the remainder of the slope divided by the compressibility group gives the aquifer. An error in the intercept therefore propagates straight into the aquifer size. The slope carries both the formation term and (cw+cf)*W, so the reservoir contribution is subtracted before W is read.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-pot-aquifer', 9, 'Two fields have both been drawn down to 2600 psia from an initial 3200 psia and are modelled with identically sized pot aquifers. The first took thirty years to get there and the second eighteen months. What influx does the pot model give each?', '["More to the first, because its aquifer has had thirty years in which to deliver water across the boundary", "More to the second, because a faster drawdown creates a larger instantaneous pressure difference at the boundary", "The same influx to each, because pot influx is a function of the current pressure and of nothing else at all", "Neither, because the pot model cannot be applied until the reservoir has produced measurable water at surface"]'::jsonb, 2, 'There is no time, rate, permeability, distance or viscosity anywhere in the pot equation. That is its definition rather than a simplification to be relaxed later, and it is why the model cannot represent a lagging aquifer or answer any question about a future rate.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-pot-aquifer', 10, 'A pot model was fitted to a tank whose influx lags. It returned an aquifer water in place of -1023276881.56074 rb and an oil in place of 313862743.836425 stb, with an empty warnings list. What should you conclude about the engine?', '["That the oil path does not check for a negative aquifer, although the gas path warns explicitly that a negative W is impossible", "That the run is valid, because the drive index closure and the fit quality checks both passed within their tolerances", "That the negative aquifer is a display convention meaning the water term was solved but not applied to the balance", "That the warnings list is only populated for runs whose validation tier falls below benchmark verified status"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The oil path''s warning block tests only whether the final drive indices miss one by more than 0.05 and whether the R-squared falls below 0.95, and this run passes both. A quiet run is not a validated run, so physical sanity on every reported quantity is your job.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-pot-aquifer', 11, 'Forcing a pot aquifer on the Ekene tank, which has none, returns which pair of values?', '["An oil in place of 12139208.1074968 stb with an R-squared of 1, since a zero aquifer falls out of the fit automatically", "An oil in place of 15218421.8713497 stb with an R-squared of 1, matching the compressibility misgrouping error", "An oil in place of 19998567.2027352 stb with an R-squared of 0.999485673716372, which is the value with Efw dropped", "An oil in place of -516449.043355256 stb, a negative volume, with an R-squared of 0.999485673716372 alongside it"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A negative oil in place and a fit statistic of 0.9995, from the same six surveys, with nothing flagged. Both are graded in the Professional capstone, at tolerances of 20000 stb and 0.002, and both are read from the panel with the aquifer selector on the pot setting.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-pot-aquifer', 12, 'That same wrong model run reports a water drive index of 1.04254388249892 and a cumulative influx of 331452.684714500 rb against a withdrawal of 317926.842484584 rb. Why do those two readings matter?', '["They indicate a strong aquifer, which is consistent with the drive mechanism the engine assigns to the run", "They are within rounding of a properly closed balance, since the drive indices still sum to exactly one", "They are physically impossible: water is credited with more than the whole withdrawal, and more water arrived than volume left", "They show that the produced water was omitted from the withdrawal, which is what pushes the water index above one"]'::jsonb, 2, 'An index above one means the oil expansion has been assigned a negative contribution, and an influx exceeding the withdrawal has nothing to fill. The indices summing to one only says the apportionment is self consistent, not that any part of it is possible.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-pot-aquifer', 13, 'On Ekene the pot plot''s horizontal coordinate, the drawdown divided by the oil expansion, is 69444.44 at every one of the six surveys, with a relative spread of 4.5e-12 percent. What does that mean for the fit?', '["That the surveys are perfectly consistent with one another, which is the strongest possible support for the fitted intercept", "That the six points are effectively one point, so the fitted slope is only the ratio of two rounding errors", "That the horizontal axis should be rescaled logarithmically to spread the six points out before fitting", "That the first survey should be excluded, since its coordinate differs from the others in the twelfth decimal place"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Ekene''s oil expansion is exactly proportional to drawdown, so their ratio is the reciprocal of that slope at every pressure. A least squares line through coincident points has no defined direction, and the R-squared measures how well a line fits the leftover floating point scatter.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-pot-aquifer', 14, 'The honest slope for a tank with no aquifer would have been 113.174770971432 rb/psi and the engine returned 295.416233943700, a difference of 182.241462972269. How does that become a 12655657.1508521 stb error in the oil in place?', '["The slope error is multiplied by the plot''s mean horizontal coordinate of 69444.44 on its way to the intercept", "The slope error is divided by the compressibility group of 0.000007 per psi, which converts it into a volume of oil", "The slope error is multiplied by the cumulative drawdown of 1103.99173733300 psi across the whole survey history", "The slope error is compounded across the six surveys, so the discrepancy accumulates by roughly a factor of six"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Every least squares line passes through the centroid, so the intercept is the mean vertical coordinate less the slope times the mean horizontal one. Reading the answer at zero when every survey sits near seventy thousand means a long extrapolation, and the lever arm is that distance.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-the-pot-aquifer', 15, 'What check should be run on every pot regression before its intercept is trusted?', '["Confirm that the fitted aquifer water in place exceeds the reservoir pore volume by at least an order of magnitude", "Confirm that the R-squared exceeds 0.999, which is the threshold below which the engine raises a regression warning", "Look at the spread of the drawdown divided by Em across the surveys, since a collapsed spread cannot separate oil from water", "Refit the same data through the origin and confirm that the forced intercept differs from the free one by less than one percent"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The plot separates the two terms only to the extent that the oil expansion departs from proportionality with drawdown. A constructed tank with a curved expansion spread its values over 0.567357187736364 percent and recovered both unknowns exactly, while Ekene''s collapsed spread returned a negative oil in place.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fetkovich', 1, 'Fetkovich models the aquifer as a tank that flows into the reservoir. Which statement is the inflow law the whole method is built on?', '["The influx rate equals a productivity index multiplied by the difference between the average aquifer pressure and the reservoir pressure at the contact", "The influx rate equals the total compressibility multiplied by the wedge pore volume and by the number of days elapsed since the start of production", "The cumulative influx equals the maximum expansible water of 211934253.721285 bbl multiplied by the ratio of the current reservoir pressure to the initial pressure", "The cumulative influx equals the aquifer pore volume multiplied by the fractional pressure drop and by the encroachment angle expressed in radians"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Fetkovich writes the aquifer exactly the way an engineer writes a well inflow performance relationship: a rate proportional to a drawdown, with the productivity index as the constant. Compressibility and pore volume fix the aquifer''s total capacity rather than its rate, and no version of the law expresses the encroachment angle in radians.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fetkovich', 2, 'The Fetkovich productivity index for a radial aquifer with a no flow outer boundary carries a denominator that is easy to mistype. With a radius ratio of 5, what is its correct value?', '["1.60943791243410, the natural logarithm of the radius ratio of 5 taken on its own with no boundary correction", "0.859437912434100, the natural logarithm of the radius ratio with 0.75 subtracted", "0.422897624804177, which is the decay term evaluated over a 365 day timestep", "2.57142857142857, which is 360 divided by the encroachment angle of 140 degrees"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The pseudo steady state form subtracts three quarters from the logarithm, so the denominator is 1.60943791243410 minus 0.75. The bare logarithm is the trap this lesson exists to prevent, while the decay term and the reciprocal of the angle fraction belong to entirely different steps of the calculation.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fetkovich', 3, 'Ahmed Example 10-10 gives k 200 md, h 100 ft, muw 0.55 cp, an encroachment angle of 140 degrees and a radius ratio of 5. What productivity index does the pseudo steady state form return?', '["62.2088067813624 bbl per day per psi", "32710.3987275100 bbl per day per psi, obtained by folding the decay term into the flow coefficient", "116.496154838747 bbl per day per psi", "77348.2677814910 bbl per day per psi, obtained from the expansible water and the initial pressure"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Assembling 0.00708 times 200 times 100 times 0.388888888888889 over 0.55 times 0.859437912434100 gives 116.496154838747, against the book''s printed 116.5. The value near 62 comes from the wrong denominator, and the two large values are the reduced marching constant and the aquifer storage per psi, both in barrels per psi rather than barrels per day per psi.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fetkovich', 4, 'An engineer computes the productivity index using ln(reD) without subtracting 0.75. What is the size and direction of the resulting error in J?', '["J falls by 46.6001200919709 percent, from 116.496154838747 to 62.2088067813624 bbl per day per psi", "J rises by 87.2663387487584 percent, because the smaller denominator makes the whole quotient larger", "J is unchanged, because the 0.75 cancels against the encroachment angle fraction in the numerator", "J falls by 25.0000000000000 percent, which is the three quarters expressed as a fraction of unity"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The two denominators stand in the ratio 1.87266338748758, and nothing else in the expression moves, so J falls by that factor to 62.2088067813624 bbl per day per psi. Dropping the 0.75 makes the denominator larger rather than smaller, and the three quarters is subtracted from a logarithm rather than applied as a percentage.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fetkovich', 5, 'Ahmed Example 10-10 prints an initial aquifer volume Wi of 28.41e9 bbl for a reservoir with a 140 degree encroachment angle. What does that printed volume represent?', '["The wedge volume already reduced by the 140 over 360 fraction, so no further scaling is needed anywhere", "The full circular ring of aquifer over all 360 degrees, with the angle fraction applied later when Wei is formed", "The maximum expansible water the aquifer can deliver, which is Wei under an alternative name used in that chapter", "The reservoir pore volume rather than the aquifer pore volume, quoted for the 9200 ft reservoir radius alone"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Computing pi times the difference of the squared radii, times thickness and porosity, and dividing by 5.615 gives 28413649389.1192 bbl, which reproduces the printed figure and is unmistakably the complete ring. The wedge share of 11049752540.2130 bbl is a separate quantity, and the expansible water of 211934253.721285 bbl is smaller again by a factor of the compressibility and the initial pressure.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fetkovich', 6, 'For the published geometry, what is the wedge share of the aquifer pore volume that the 140 degree contact actually represents?', '["28413649389.1192 bbl, since the aquifer radius of 46000 ft already accounts for the partial contact", "211934253.721285 bbl, which is the volume of water available to flow across the 140 degree arc", "82418876.4471666 bbl, which is what the geometry gives once the angle has been correctly applied", "11049752540.2130 bbl, which is the full circle volume multiplied by 0.388888888888889"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The angle fraction is 140 divided by 360, or 0.388888888888889, and applying it once to the full circle volume gives the wedge. The value near 211.9 million barrels is the expansible water rather than a pore volume, and the value near 82.4 million barrels is what appears when the angle fraction is applied a second time by mistake.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fetkovich', 7, 'How is Wei, the maximum water the Fetkovich aquifer can ever deliver by expansion, formed from the geometry?', '["By multiplying the full circle pore volume by the total compressibility and by the initial pressure, leaving the angle out entirely", "By multiplying the wedge pore volume by the productivity index and by the length of the first timestep in days", "By multiplying the wedge pore volume by the total compressibility and by the initial pressure, giving 211934253.721285 bbl", "By multiplying the full circle pore volume by the decay term of 0.422897624804177 and by the radius ratio of 5"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Expansible water is compressibility times pore volume times pressure, and the pore volume that belongs in it is the wedge, so 0.000007 times 11049752540.2130 times 2740 gives 211934253.721285 bbl against the printed 211.9 million. The productivity index governs the rate rather than the capacity, and the decay term governs how much of that capacity moves within a step.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fetkovich', 8, 'A reader takes the printed Wi of 28.41e9 bbl to be the wedge volume and multiplies by 140 over 360 again before forming Wei. By what factor is the resulting Wei wrong?', '["It is 1.87266338748758 times too small, which is the ratio of the two candidate productivity index denominators", "It is 2.00000000000000 times too small, because the doubled fraction always halves the expansible water", "It is 1.61803398874989 times too small, an artefact of the ratio of the aquifer radius to the reservoir radius", "It is 2.57142857142857 times too small, which is exactly 360 divided by 140, and Wei comes out at 82418876.4471666 bbl"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Applying the angle fraction twice divides the answer by 0.388888888888889 once too often, and the reciprocal of that fraction is 360 over 140, or 2.57142857142857. Marching the influx with the shrunken value drops the cumulative water influx to 19.6811125122705 MMbbl, an error of 48.1709833750379 percent.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fetkovich', 9, 'In the marching equation the decay term is written as one minus the exponential of minus J times pi times dt over Wei. What does that term measure?', '["The fraction of the water that the available drawdown entitles the aquifer to deliver which actually has time to arrive within the timestep", "The fraction of the aquifer pore volume that lies inside the 140 degree encroachment arc rather than outside it, which here is 0.388888888888889", "The fraction by which the reservoir pressure falls across the timestep, relative to the initial reservoir pressure", "The fraction of the total compressibility contributed by the connate water rather than by the rock framework"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The exponential is the analytic integration of the inflow law across a step of fixed reservoir pressure. Over an infinitely long step the term approaches one and the aquifer fully equilibrates, while over a very short step it approaches zero and almost nothing moves. For the published case with a 365 day step it is 0.422897624804177, against a printed 0.4229.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fetkovich', 10, 'For a uniform 365 day timestep the storage constant and the decay term can be folded into a single reduced marching constant. What is its value for the published case, and what is the first step influx?', '["77348.2677814910 bbl per psi, giving a first step influx of 9281792.13377892 bbl over the 120 psi drawdown", "32710.3987275100 bbl per psi, giving a first step influx of 3925247.84730120 bbl over the 120 psi drawdown", "0.00150612493569796 bbl per psi, giving a first step influx of 0.180734992283755 bbl over the 120 psi drawdown", "211934253.721285 bbl per psi, giving a first step influx of 25432110446.5542 bbl over the 120 psi drawdown"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Multiplying the storage constant of 77348.2677814910 bbl per psi by the decay term of 0.422897624804177 gives 32710.3987275100 bbl per psi, and the book prints 32705 because it multiplied its own rounded intermediates. Times the 120 psi drawdown that gives 3925247.84730120 bbl, matching the printed 3.925 MMbbl for the first step.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fetkovich', 11, 'After the first step delivers 3925247.84730120 bbl, what happens to the average aquifer pressure, and why?', '["It stays at 2740 psia, because a Fetkovich aquifer only loses pressure once the cumulative influx exceeds Wei", "It drops to 2620 psia, matching the midpoint reservoir pressure that drove the step to its own equilibrium", "It drops to 2689.25228502350 psia, because the aquifer has spent 0.0185210638600370 of its expansible water", "It drops to 2500 psia, following the end of step reservoir pressure with a one step lag built into the scheme"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The scheme updates the aquifer as pi times one minus the ratio of cumulative influx to Wei, so spending 1.85 percent of the capacity costs 1.85 percent of the initial pressure and leaves 2689.25228502350 psia, printed by the book as 2689. This running down is what makes the aquifer weaken as it works and what limits the total influx without any external cap.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fetkovich', 12, 'The published solution drives step 1 with a reservoir pressure of 2620 psia, although the history reads 2740 psia at the start of the year and 2500 psia at the end. Where does 2620 come from?', '["It is the pressure at which the aquifer and the reservoir would come into equilibrium if the step were run to completion", "It is the initial pressure reduced by the decay term applied to the full 240 psi drop recorded across the step", "It is the geometric mean of the two survey pressures, chosen so that the influx is proportional to the pressure ratio", "It is the midpoint of the two survey pressures, since the driving pressure is the average across the step rather than at an instant"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The convention is that the reservoir pressure driving step n is the arithmetic average of the pressures at the two ends of the step, which for the first step is 2740 plus 2500 over two. The four midpoints of 2620, 2395, 2199.5 and 2029 reproduce the book''s own printed column, and the engine implements the same rule.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fetkovich', 13, 'An engineer uses the end of step reservoir pressure instead of the midpoint. What happens to the computed water influx?', '["It is overstated, because the lower pressure gives a larger drawdown, and the cumulative influx rises by 16.3893300978283 percent", "It is understated, because early influx is suppressed until the reservoir pressure has had time to fall away from the initial value", "It is unchanged in total, because the aquifer pressure update absorbs the difference before the next step begins", "It is understated at first and overstated later, so the cumulative influx after four steps happens to land on the printed value"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The end of step pressure is the lowest of the candidates, so it produces the largest drawdown and the most water. In the first step it doubles the influx exactly, from 3925247.84730120 to 7850495.69460241 bbl, and after four steps the cumulative reaches 44.1967000350130 MMbbl against the correct 37.9731544101719 MMbbl. The frequent claim that this choice understates early influx has the direction backwards.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fetkovich', 14, 'What goes wrong in the very first step if the start of step reservoir pressure is used as the driving pressure?', '["The drawdown becomes negative, so the model reports water leaving the reservoir and flowing back into the aquifer until the second step begins", "The drawdown is zero, since the aquifer and the reservoir both sit at 2740 psia, so the model delivers no water at all in the first year", "The drawdown is the full 240 psi, so the first step delivers twice the water the published solution reports for it", "The drawdown is undefined until the aquifer pressure has been updated at least once by a completed timestep"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both the reservoir and the aquifer begin at the initial pressure, so this choice produces exactly zero influx in a year during which the reservoir actually fell 240 psi. Every later step inherits the deficit and the cumulative after four steps reaches only 31.7496087853307 MMbbl, low by 16.3893300978283 percent, which mirrors the error the end of step choice makes in the opposite direction.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-fetkovich', 15, 'The engine''s marching scheme returns 37.9731544101719 MMbbl of cumulative influx after four steps against a printed 37.971 MMbbl. How should that comparison be reported?', '["As a failure to reproduce the benchmark, since a validated engine should match a published table exactly", "As evidence that the engine uses a different aquifer model from the one the published solution used", "As agreement of 0.00567383048077399 percent, with the residual attributable to the book rounding its intermediates", "As agreement that cannot be assessed, because the book does not print any cumulative influx column to compare against"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The relative difference is five parts in a hundred thousand, and it is made of the book rounding its aquifer pressures to whole psi and its step influxes to three or four figures before reusing them. When the author''s intermediates are not visible, the honest standard is agreement to within about one unit in the last printed place, and every entry in this table passes that test.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-gas-and-p-over-z', 1, 'In the gas material balance the rock and connate water term uses a compressibility grouping that is often mistyped. For Swi 0.15, cw 0.000003 per psi and cf 0.000006 per psi, which grouping and value are correct?', '["Swi times the sum of cw and cf, all over one minus Swi, giving 0.00000158823529411765 per psi", "The sum of cw and cf divided by Swi, giving 0.0000600000000000000 per psi", "Swi times cw plus cf, all over one minus Swi, giving 0.00000758823529411765 per psi", "Swi times cw plus cf, all over Swi, giving 0.0000430000000000000 per psi"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Connate water expands in proportion to the pore space it occupies, so only cw carries Swi, while rock compaction shrinks the whole pore volume regardless of what is in it. Bracketing both compressibilities inside the saturation gives 0.00000158823529411765 per psi, less than a quarter of the correct group, and the same trap appears on the oil side of the balance.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-gas-and-p-over-z', 2, 'At year ten of the published gas case the rock and connate water term carries 2.67127028078238 percent of the total expansion, while the undersaturated oil tank of the Associate tier carried 39.2996108949418 percent. What accounts for the difference?', '["Gas is enormously compressible, so its own expansion dominates the balance and leaves the rock and water term a small correction", "The gas case was run with a lower formation compressibility, since cf of 0.000006 per psi is smaller than the value used for the oil tank", "The gas case has an aquifer supplying part of the drive, and the presence of any aquifer reduces the rock and water term proportionally", "The gas case covers a smaller pressure drop, so the term, which is linear in the pressure drop, has had less opportunity to grow"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The formation volume factor of this gas rises from 0.6279 to 1.2829 RB per Mscf, a factor of 2.04315973881191, and that expansion is what produces the field. The gas case actually uses a larger formation compressibility than the oil tank and covers a much larger pressure drop, so neither of those explanations survives contact with the numbers.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-gas-and-p-over-z', 3, 'The engine''s gas input field for the formation volume factor is named bg_rb_mscf, and the source comments insist on the unit. Why does that matter so much?', '["Because the correlation path cannot be reached at all unless the lab value is supplied in reservoir barrels per Mscf, which the precedence chain checks before it interpolates", "Because loading the factor in reservoir barrels per scf moves the gas in place by three orders of magnitude with no warning raised", "Because reservoir barrels per Mscf is the only unit in which the compressibility factor z remains dimensionless", "Because the drive indices are computed per Mscf while the expansions are computed per scf, and the two must be reconciled"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine carries the expansions internally per standard cubic foot while lab data arrives per thousand standard cubic feet, so a mislabelled column is a clean factor of a thousand in the answer. Nothing about the unit affects whether z is dimensionless, and the engine converts consistently rather than mixing the two bases.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-gas-and-p-over-z', 4, 'Running the published gas case with its pot aquifer gives drive indices at year ten of 0.941809113312943, 0.0258487571123217 and 0.0328166271192876. How should that split be read?', '["The three indices are within rounding of one third each, which is the signature of a balanced drive in which no single mechanism dominates the others", "Water influx dominates at 94 percent, and the aquifer should be classified as strong on that evidence alone", "The indices sum to 1.00047449754455, which exceeds unity and therefore indicates a material balance failure", "Gas expansion does about 94 percent of the work, water influx about 3.3 percent, and rock and water about 2.6 percent"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The largest index is the gas expansion term, and the engine classifies the aquifer as weak on that evidence. The sum of 1.00047449754455 is a closure to within five parts in ten thousand rather than a failure, and Pletcher''s own published indices of 0.942, 0.033 and 0.026 agree to the last figure he printed.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-gas-and-p-over-z', 5, 'For a volumetric gas tank, p over z falls linearly with cumulative production. What does the x intercept of that line represent?', '["The gas originally in place, since p over z reaches zero only when the cumulative production equals the whole tank", "The abandonment pressure divided by the compressibility factor at abandonment conditions", "The recoverable gas at the economic limit, which is always a fixed fraction of the gas originally in place once the abandonment pressure has been fixed", "The slope of the line multiplied by the initial value of p over z, which is how the tank size is recovered"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Rearranging the fixed container statement gives p over z equal to its initial value times one minus the ratio of cumulative production to gas in place, so the plotted variable reaches zero exactly when the production reaches the tank size. Abandonment conditions and recovery fractions are economic quantities that this plot knows nothing about.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-gas-and-p-over-z', 6, 'The published gas case has an initial p over z of 5728.19871336669 psia and a true gas in place of 100.8 Bcf. At a cumulative production of 54.75 Bcf, a volumetric tank would sit at 2616.90030506484 psia, but the measured value is 2803.69858645977 psia. What does that tell you?', '["The compressibility factor at that pressure has been misreported, since a volumetric prediction cannot be wrong by that margin and the tabulated z of 0.9409 must therefore be in error", "The tank is producing more gas than the volumetric line allows, so the true gas in place must be smaller than 100.8 Bcf", "The pressure is being held up by 186.798281394931 psia by something outside the closed tank model, so the tank is not volumetric", "The reservoir is still in transient flow, so the tank average pressure has not yet stabilised and the comparison is premature"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The volumetric reference line is a prediction made from the initial survey and the known gas in place alone, without touching the intervening data, so a measured point sitting above it is direct evidence that some mechanism is supporting the pressure. In this case it is the aquifer, and the gap in percentage terms grows steadily to 7.13815046883500 percent by year ten.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-gas-and-p-over-z', 7, 'Water influx into a gas reservoir bends the p over z trend. In which direction does it bend, and what does that do to a gas booking read off the bent line as if it were straight?', '["Downward, and the resulting gas in place is understated, which makes the naive reading a conservative one", "Upward, because the pressure is held up, and the extrapolation travels further to the axis so the gas in place is overstated", "Upward at early time and downward at late time, so the two effects cancel over a long enough history", "Neither, since influx changes only the intercept of the line and leaves its slope, and therefore the gas in place, at the true 100.8 Bcf"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Water occupies pore space the gas used to occupy, so the gas is confined more tightly and its pressure falls less per unit produced. The trend is therefore shallower than a closed tank would give, and every step of that chain runs the same way, which means there is no version of this error that is conservative.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-gas-and-p-over-z', 8, 'Fitting a straight line to all eleven measured p over z points of the published case and extrapolating to the axis gives what apparent gas in place, and with what quality of fit?', '["100.994684584637 Bcf with an R squared of 0.999703243147068, which is what a least squares line through the measured column returns", "104.984995886491 Bcf with an R squared of 0.999764886431655, obtained from the raw measured column", "114.976909090015 Bcf with an R squared of 0.999907270407081, which is what the whole history supports", "107.924344535358 Bcf with an R squared of 0.999329425941439, which is 7.06780211841055 percent above the truth"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The naive fit has a slope of 53.4643621104832 psia per Bcf and an intercept of 5770.10623677492 psia, so the axis crossing is at 107.924344535358 Bcf against a true 100.8 Bcf. The value near 115 Bcf comes from fitting only the first four years, the value near 105 Bcf from a compaction corrected column, and the value near 101 Bcf from modelling the aquifer explicitly.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-gas-and-p-over-z', 9, 'The eleven point straight line fit returns an intercept of 5770.10623677492 psia while the measured initial p over z is 5728.19871336669 psia. Why is that comparison worth making on every gas plot?', '["Because the fitted line of a closed tank must pass through its own initial condition, so a miss of 41.9075234082293 psia upward is a model independent warning", "Because the difference between the two values is the water influx expressed in psia, which can be converted directly into reservoir barrels once the aquifer compressibility is known", "Because the fitted intercept replaces the measured value once enough history has accumulated to make the regression reliable", "Because an intercept above the measured value is the normal consequence of least squares and confirms that the fit is unbiased"]'::jsonb, 0, 'You know one point on the line exactly, from the initial survey, and the fit does not use that knowledge as a constraint. When the fitted intercept sits above the anchor, the trend through the later points is too shallow for the tank to be closed, and the check costs one subtraction and needs no aquifer model at all.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-gas-and-p-over-z', 10, 'The naive line overstates the gas in place of the published case by 7.06780211841055 percent. By how much does it overstate the gas remaining to be produced at year ten, and why?', '["By the same 7.06780211841055 percent, since a proportional error in the tank size scales every quantity derived from it equally, the remaining reserves included", "By less than 7 percent, because the gas already produced is known exactly and dilutes the error across the whole booking", "By 15.4708893275957 percent, because the whole error lands on the part not yet produced, which is only 46.05 Bcf of the true tank", "By an amount that cannot be determined without knowing the abandonment pressure and the economic limit of the field"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Cumulative production of 54.75 Bcf is metered and not in dispute, so the difference between the apparent 107.924344535358 Bcf and the true 100.8 Bcf falls entirely into the remainder. That turns a 7 percent error in the tank into a 15 percent error in the reserves, and the concentration gets worse the further into depletion the mistake is made.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-gas-and-p-over-z', 11, 'The pot aquifer plot for a gas reservoir plots F over Eg against the pressure drop over Eg. Where do the two unknowns appear?', '["Both appear in the slope, and the intercept is fixed at zero by the requirement that the line pass through the initial condition", "The gas in place is the intercept in scf, and the aquifer water in place is recovered from the slope after subtracting the known expansion term", "The gas in place is the slope in scf per psi, and the aquifer water in place is the intercept read directly in reservoir barrels", "The gas in place is the x intercept as on a p over z plot, and the aquifer water in place is the y intercept of the same line"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This arrangement of the balance puts the gas in place at the y intercept directly and buries the aquifer size in the slope alongside a rock and connate water expansion term, so the engine subtracts that term and divides by the sum of the water and formation compressibilities. It is a different rearrangement from the p over z plot and the unknowns land in different places.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-gas-and-p-over-z', 12, 'Which set of tolerances is the published gas case held to when it is run with a pot aquifer?', '["Gas in place within 2 percent of 100.8 Bcf, aquifer water within 10 percent of 74.5 MM res bbl, influx within 10 percent of 2494000 res bbl, and the drive index sum within 0.05 of unity", "Every reported quantity within 0.5 percent of the simulator value, since the case is a synthetic model with exactly known answers and a synthetic model leaves no room anywhere for measurement error", "Gas in place within 10 percent and every other quantity reported for information only, with no numerical gate applied to them", "Gas in place within 2 percent and an R squared above 0.9999, with the aquifer quantities left ungated because they are not observable"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The gates are looser on the aquifer quantities than on the gas in place because the aquifer is inferred from a slope while the gas is read from an intercept. The run passes all four, returning 100.994684584637 Bcf, 69.0397563415655 MM res bbl, 2344383.00609054 res bbl and a sum of 1.00047449754455, and no gate anywhere in the case is set on R squared.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-gas-and-p-over-z', 13, 'The published gas case run without any aquifer term returns an R squared of 0.999929244287833, while the same case run with a pot aquifer returns 0.999703243147068. What follows from that?', '["The volumetric run is the better model, since a higher R squared over the same data is what model selection is based on", "The two runs are statistically indistinguishable, so the choice between them should be made on which is simpler to defend", "The R squared values must have been computed on different regressions, which makes any comparison between them meaningless", "Nothing about which model is right, because the run with the better fit is 17.0032474579172 times further from the true gas in place"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Goodness of fit measures consistency between a model and the data it was given, and a wrong model with a free parameter can absorb a real physical effect into that parameter and fit beautifully. The volumetric run misses the truth by 3.28399818335935 percent while the aquifer run misses by 0.193139468886000 percent, so the fit statistic ranked them backwards.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-gas-and-p-over-z', 14, 'What is the Ramagost and Farshad correction for, and what happens when it is applied to a reservoir whose p over z plot is bent by water rather than by compaction?', '["It corrects for the difference between wellhead and datum pressure, and applying it to a water drive case leaves the fitted intercept unchanged", "It corrects an overpressured gas tank for rock compaction, and on this water drive case it improves the answer to 104.984995886491 Bcf while leaving it 4.15178163342327 percent high", "It corrects the compressibility factor for non hydrocarbon impurities, and on this case it moves the apparent gas in place further from the truth", "It corrects for water production reported at the surface rather than in reservoir barrels, and on this case it recovers the true 100.8 Bcf exactly"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In an overpressured reservoir the rock is held apart by the initial pressure and compacts as the field depletes, which supports the pressure the way an aquifer does, so the correction multiplies the plotted variable by one minus cf times the pressure drop over one minus Swi. Applied to a bend that water caused, it pulls the line the right way but can only remove as much as cf can explain, which is why a correction that improves your answer is not thereby the correct correction.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-gas-and-p-over-z', 15, 'The engine''s inputs carry a solver_method field with the values havlena_odeh, p_over_z, p_over_z_modified and pot_aquifer_plot. What does setting that field actually do on the gas path?', '["It selects the regression the gas path will run, so changing the label from p_over_z to pot_aquifer_plot changes the answer", "It selects the validation tier the run will be reported at, independently of the arithmetic the engine performs", "It records the method you intend, while the arithmetic is selected by the aquifer model you set on the same inputs", "It selects the pressure convention used inside the marching scheme when a time dependent aquifer has been requested"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In the engine source the field is declared on the input type and never branched on, and the gas path chooses its regression from the aquifer model instead. The field belongs in your run documentation as a statement of intent, but changing it on its own will not change a single number the engine returns.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-pvt-and-precedence', 1, 'At each timestep the material balance engine needs an oil formation volume factor. In what order does it look for one?', '["Per-row values in the production data first, then interpolation in the PVT lab table, then a correlation", "A correlation first, then the PVT lab table, and per-row production values only as a final fallback", "The PVT lab table first, then per-row values in the production data, then whichever correlation is selected", "Whichever of the three available sources produces the smoothest curve of the factor against pressure"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The chain runs from most specific to most general. A value attached to the row wins, a laboratory table interpolated at that row''s pressure comes next, and a published correlation is what is left when neither is available.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-pvt-and-precedence', 2, 'An oil production row carries bo_rb_stb but no rs_scf_stb. What does the engine do with that row''s oil formation volume factor?', '["It uses the supplied factor and computes the solution gas ratio from the selected correlation for that row alone", "It uses the supplied factor and holds the solution gas ratio at its initial value for the remainder of the history", "It ignores the supplied factor, because the per-row path is taken only when both properties are present on the row", "It rejects the whole case with an error naming the row and the missing solution gas ratio column"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The timestep loop takes the per-row branch only when the row carries both the solution gas ratio and the oil formation volume factor. Supplying one of the two leaves the row dropping through to the laboratory table or a correlation, which surprises people who assume the engine will use whatever it is given.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-pvt-and-precedence', 3, 'A PVT lab table spans 2500 to 4000 psia. A survey sits at 2100 psia and the row carries no per-row PVT. What happens?', '["The engine extrapolates the table linearly past its lowest stored pressure row and records that extrapolation in the result", "The lookup returns nothing, the engine uses a correlation, and it warns that the pressure is outside the table", "The engine clamps the lookup to the value stored at the table''s lowest pressure and continues without comment", "The run stops with an error, since a lab table that does not span the history cannot be used at all"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Pressures outside the range a table spans return nothing from the interpolation, and the engine then uses whichever correlation the case selected. The fall-through is quiet in the numbers and loud only in the warning list, which is the whole reason the warning exists.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-pvt-and-precedence', 4, 'The two phase formation volume factor is Bt = Bo + Bg(Rsi - Rs). What does it reduce to when the reservoir pressure is over the bubble point?', '["It reduces to Bo exactly, because no gas has yet come out of solution and the bracket is zero", "It reduces to Bo multiplied by the ratio of the initial solution gas ratio to the current one", "It reduces to Bg times the initial solution gas ratio, since the oil term becomes negligible there", "It stays larger than Bo by the volume of free gas dissolved in the connate water at that pressure"]'::jsonb, 0, 'With no free gas in the reservoir the solution gas ratio still equals its initial value, the bracket vanishes and the two phase factor and the oil factor are the same number. That is why an undersaturated tank never needs a gas formation volume factor.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-pvt-and-precedence', 5, 'The Ekene fixture has a bubble point of 2000 psia and its lowest survey pressure is 2096.00826266700 psia. How far is the tank from saturating at the last survey?', '["2096.00826266700 psi, since the distance is measured from the tank''s initial pressure of 3200 psia", "1103.99173733300 psi, which is the total drawdown accumulated across the six surveys", "96.0082626669955 psi, and the tank is therefore undersaturated at every survey in the history", "It cannot be stated, because the bubble point moves as the solution gas ratio falls"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Subtracting the bubble point from the lowest survey pressure leaves 96.0082626669955 psi of margin. Every survey in the history sits over the bubble point, which is why the solution gas ratio holds at 400 scf/stb throughout.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-pvt-and-precedence', 6, 'When the correlation path computes an undersaturated oil formation volume factor, where does the oil compressibility come from?', '["From a Vasquez-Beggs correlation evaluated at the case''s gravity, gas gravity and reservoir temperature", "From the case inputs, which require an oil compressibility whenever a bubble point is supplied", "From a hard coded placeholder constant of 1e-5 per psi, flagged as such in a comment in the source", "From the slope of the supplied lab table between the two rows bracketing the bubble point pressure"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The undersaturated branch multiplies the bubble point factor by one minus a compressibility times the pressure excess, and that compressibility is a fixed 1e-5 per psi with a source comment calling it a placeholder until a later phase needs a proper correlation.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-pvt-and-precedence', 7, 'The Ekene fixture was designed with an oil compressibility of 1.2e-5 per psi. How does the engine''s placeholder compare?', '["It is 16.6666666666667 percent lower than the value the fixture''s own pressure history was built from", "It is 20.0000000000000 percent higher, which is why the correlation path overstates the oil in place", "It matches to within a rounding difference, so the two paths differ only in the initial factor they use", "It is two orders of magnitude smaller, which is why the undersaturated branch barely changes the factor"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The placeholder is 1e-5 per psi against a design value of 1.2e-5 per psi, a shortfall of 16.6666666666667 percent. That single constant sets the shape of the oil formation volume factor against pressure whenever no per-row value is supplied.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-pvt-and-precedence', 8, 'Strip the per-row fluid properties off the Ekene surveys and the engine returns 13296089.9738372 stb where the committed fixture gives 12139208.1074968 stb. What is the error?', '["9.53012631545501 percent high, on a dataset in which not one pressure and not one produced volume changed", "9.53012631545501 percent low, because the correlation understates how much the oil swells on depletion", "1156881.86634040 percent, once the absolute gap is expressed against the total expansion at the last survey", "Under one percent, since the fit statistic on the stripped run stays a fraction below one at 0.999999978398381"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The gap of 1156881.86634040 stb against the true oil in place works out at 9.53012631545501 percent, and the answer is too large. Not one pressure or cumulative volume differs between the two runs, so the entire error comes from the fluid property chain.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-pvt-and-precedence', 9, 'On an undersaturated tank with no per-row PVT, why does supplying a good lab table barely move the oil in place the engine reports?', '["Because the regression weights the outermost surveys so heavily that fluid properties in the interior of the history cannot reach the slope", "Because the bubble point factor scales withdrawal and expansion alike, so it cancels from the slope and only the placeholder compressibility survives", "Because the table is interpolated at the initial pressure only, and the engine ignores every other row of it", "Because the total expansion term is dominated by rock and connate water, which no lab table can supply"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Over the bubble point the withdrawal, the oil expansion and the rock and water term are all proportional to the bubble point factor, so it drops out of the ratio the slope reads. What the slope does see is the compressibility that sets how the factor changes with pressure, and that is the placeholder.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-pvt-and-precedence', 10, 'Running the Ekene fixture exactly as committed produces five warnings. What do they say?', '["That five survey pressures fall outside the range the supplied PVT lab table spans", "That the drive index sum at the final timestep sits outside the expected band of one plus or minus 0.05", "That five production rows are missing a solution gas ratio and were resolved by correlation instead", "That the PVT lab table is not sorted ascending, one warning for each adjacent pair of rows"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The fixture lists its laboratory table in descending pressure, the way a lab report prints one, and the engine''s structural check expects each pressure to exceed the one before it. Six rows give five adjacent pairs and five identically shaped complaints.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-pvt-and-precedence', 11, 'A laboratory table listed in descending pressure order is not merely untidy. What does the interpolation routine actually do with it?', '["It sorts the rows internally before interpolating, so the descending order costs nothing but the warnings", "It reads the first row''s pressure as the bottom of the range and the last as the top, so every lookup falls outside", "It interpolates in reverse order and returns values that are right in magnitude but attached to the wrong pressures throughout", "It uses only the two rows closest to the initial pressure and treats the rest of the table as absent"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The range test compares the target against the first and last stored pressures. On a descending table that reads as a range running from the highest value up to the lowest, which nothing can be inside, so the table is inert and every property comes from a correlation instead.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-pvt-and-precedence', 12, 'Force a pot aquifer onto the Ekene tank and the engine reports an oil in place of -516449.043355256 stb. What does its validation tier read?', '["It drops to engineering basis, because a negative oil in place cannot be a benchmarked result", "It reads published method, the tier the engine assigns whenever a regression returns a negative slope", "It is suppressed entirely, and the result carries a warning naming the impossible oil in place instead", "It reads benchmark verified, unchanged, because the tier describes the method rather than this run"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The tier is a statement about whether the oil and pot aquifer path has been checked against a published worked example. It is not a statement about whether your case should have used that path, and nothing in the output flags the choice except the negative sign and your own judgment.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-pvt-and-precedence', 13, 'A history has one observation date out of order. Under which aquifer setting does the run still complete?', '["Under Fetkovich, which recovers the step length from the pressure history when the dates disagree", "Under none of them, since the date check runs before the aquifer model is consulted at all", "Under Carter-Tracy, which superposes over pressure changes rather than over elapsed time", "Under the pot aquifer, which is time independent and never asks for a step length between rows"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Extracting the time steps raises a named error on a missing or non increasing date, and only the time marching models call it. A pot aquifer responds instantly and has no clock, so the same broken date passes through it without comment.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-pvt-and-precedence', 14, 'With the per-row properties removed, the engine sources an initial solution gas ratio of 421.939227522706 scf/stb where the fixture states 400. What does that gap represent?', '["A unit conversion, since the correlation returns the ratio in standard cubic feet per reservoir barrel rather than per stock tank barrel", "A 5.48480688067650 percent difference between a general answer about typical fluids and a specific one about this fluid", "An extrapolation warning, because 400 scf/stb sits outside the correlation''s documented training range", "Random scatter, which averages out across the six surveys and leaves the slope unaffected"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The correlation predicts what a fluid of that gravity, gas gravity and temperature would typically dissolve, and Ekene''s fluid is not typical. The 21.9392275227060 scf/stb difference is a bias rather than scatter, so it stays in every term it touches.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-pvt-and-precedence', 15, 'You are handed a run that reports five lab table sort warnings, per-row PVT on every production row and a benchmark tier. What is the right response?', '["Reject the result and rerun it once the laboratory table has been sorted into ascending order, since warnings invalidate a run", "Accept it silently, because a benchmark tier means the engine has confirmed that the inputs were well formed", "Note that the table was never reached, since per-row values sit higher in the chain, and say so in the memo", "Report the result with the warnings attached and no comment, so the reviewer can decide what they mean"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The warnings are true and specific, and they describe a part of the file the run never consulted. Sorting the table changes nothing in the answer and clears the list, but the useful move is to explain in the memo why the complaint does not touch the number.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 1, 'The Professional reading is organised into three phases. What are they, in order?', '["Fit the line, choose the aquifer that improves the fit statistic most, then document the parameters you settled on", "Diagnose, then model, then defend, and no phase is allowed to borrow work from the phase that follows it", "Assemble the data, compute every aquifer model available, then report the one whose indices close nearest to one", "Model, then diagnose whether the model was appropriate, then defend the choice to a reviewer in writing"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The order is the protection. Diagnosing after a model is on the screen turns evidence into justification, and defending a reading you never ran without the aquifer leaves you with nothing to compare against.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 2, 'Why must the drift in the ratio of withdrawal to total expansion be read before an aquifer model is chosen?', '["Because the ratio cannot be computed once an aquifer term has been added to the withdrawal side of the balance", "Because the drift determines the encroachment angle and the aquifer radius that the model will need as inputs", "Because the regression will refuse to converge if an aquifer model is supplied before the ratio has been inspected", "Because the drift is the only evidence about the tank that does not depend on the model you are about to pick"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every number that comes out after a model is selected has that model built into it. A ratio column read from withdrawal and expansion alone is the one piece of the argument a reviewer can weigh without accepting your choice first.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 3, 'In the Fetkovich constants, what turns the connected wedge volume into the encroachable water Wei?', '["Multiplying by the total compressibility and by the initial pressure of the aquifer", "Multiplying by the encroachment angle fraction a second time, then by the total compressibility", "Dividing by the productivity index and by the length of one time step", "Multiplying by the porosity and dividing by the conversion factor from cubic feet to barrels"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The aquifer cannot deliver its whole volume, only the part that expands as its pressure falls to zero. On the Ahmed 10-10 geometry that turns 11049752540.2130 bbl of connected water into 211934253.721285 bbl of encroachable water, under two percent of it.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 4, 'An engineer applies the 140 degree encroachment angle both when quoting Wi and again when forming Wei. What does that cost?', '["Nothing, since the encroachment angle cancels out of the marching scheme once the productivity index is formed", "It halves the encroachable water and leaves the productivity index untouched", "It makes Wei 2.57142857142857 times too small, at 82418876.4471665 bbl instead of 211934253.721285 bbl", "It doubles the encroachable water, because the angle fraction is applied to a reciprocal in the second place"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The angle fraction of 0.388888888888889 belongs to the step that takes the connected wedge out of the full circle, and nowhere else. Applying it twice divides the encroachable water by that fraction a second time, which is a factor of 2.57142857142857.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 5, 'Ahmed Example 10-10 prints an aquifer volume of 28410000000 bbl. What geometry does that number describe?', '["The wedge actually in contact with the reservoir, already reduced by the encroachment angle", "The full circular ring between the reservoir radius and the aquifer radius, with no angle applied", "The encroachable water, that is the ring volume already multiplied by compressibility and pressure", "The pore volume of the reservoir itself, which the aquifer surrounds over 140 degrees of its circumference"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Computing the ring from the published radii, thickness and porosity gives 28413649389.1192 bbl, which matches the printed figure to 0.0128454386453786 percent. The angle enters afterwards, when the connected wedge is taken.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 6, 'Replace the pseudo steady state denominator in the productivity index with plain ln(reD). What happens on the Ahmed 10-10 geometry?', '["The index falls 46.6001200919709 percent to 62.2088067813624 bbl/d/psi and every influx step shrinks with it", "The index rises to 62.2088067813624 bbl/d/psi and the influx is overstated in every step of the march", "The index is unchanged, because the difference between the two groups is absorbed by the encroachable water term", "The index falls by roughly one percent, which is smaller than the tolerance the capstone grades against"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The pseudo steady state group is 0.859437912434100 against 1.60943791243410 for the plain logarithm, and the index is inversely proportional to it. The four step cumulative influx falls with it, from 37.9731544101719 MMbbl to 26.6727996152478 MMbbl.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 7, 'The Fetkovich decay term over a 365 day step on the Ahmed 10-10 aquifer is 0.422897624804177. What does that number mean?', '["The fraction of the aquifer''s encroachable water that has been produced by the end of the first step", "The fraction by which the aquifer pressure falls during the step, measured against its initial pressure", "The fraction of the available drawdown that the aquifer actually delivers water against during one step", "The fraction of the reservoir''s own pressure drop that is supplied by water rather than by expansion"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The term is one minus an exponential in the productivity index, the initial pressure, the step length and the encroachable water. It says how much of the pressure difference driving the step is converted into influx before the step ends, and the book prints 0.4229 for it.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 8, 'Use the end of step reservoir pressure instead of the midpoint in the Fetkovich march on Ahmed 10-10. What happens to the cumulative influx?', '["It falls to 26.6727996152478 MMbbl, because the smaller drawdown per step depletes the aquifer more slowly", "It is unchanged after four steps, because the marching scheme corrects the aquifer pressure at every step", "It rises 16.3893300978283 percent to 44.1967000350130 MMbbl, and the first step alone doubles in size", "It rises by exactly the ratio of the first step''s midpoint drawdown to its end of step drawdown"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The end of step pressure is lower than the midpoint, so the drawdown driving every step is larger. The first step goes from 3.92524784730120 MMbbl to 7.85049569460241 MMbbl and the four step total finishes 16.3893300978283 percent high.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 9, 'The pot aquifer forced onto Ekene returns an oil in place of -516449.043355256 stb with a fit statistic of 0.999485673716372. What does the pairing teach?', '["That the fit statistic is unreliable whenever an aquifer term is present in the regression at all", "That the six Ekene surveys are too few to constrain a two parameter regression to a physical answer", "That the pot aquifer is unsuitable for oil tanks and should only ever be applied to gas material balance cases", "That a regression will spend a freedom it is given, and a high fit statistic is no evidence of a correct model"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The data needs one parameter and the model was given two, so the aquifer term absorbed 42890161.1573930 rb of imagined water and the oil in place followed it past zero. Nothing about the fit resisted, because a good fit measures internal consistency and never correctness.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 10, 'On the pot aquifer run of Ekene the drive indices sum to exactly 1.00000000000000. What does that tell you about the model?', '["That the aquifer term was sized correctly, since an influx volume that was wrong would break the closure", "That the run used the net withdrawal convention, which is the only one of the two conventions that can close to exactly one", "Nothing about correctness: the water drive index of 1.04254388249892 just absorbs what a negative oil in place leaves", "That the indices were computed at the final timestep only, where the closure is enforced by construction"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Dividing the reported oil in place by the true 12139208.1074968 stb gives -0.0425438824989180, exactly the depletion and rock and water indices added together, and the water drive index is one minus that. Closure is an identity here, not a test.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 11, 'A defence of an aquifer model has to make four things visible to a reviewer. Which set is it?', '["The drift, the geometry behind the constants, the convention used, and the answer without the aquifer", "The fit statistic, the drive index closure, the aquifer strength classification and the validation tier", "The software version, the solver method, the number of iterations and the convergence tolerance reached", "The oil in place, the cumulative influx, the encroachable water and the final aquifer pressure"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The test is whether a reviewer can reach your conclusion from the document without rerunning anything. Evidence, inputs with sources, bookkeeping decisions, and the counterfactual are what that takes, and none of them can be read off a results screen.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 12, 'Why does the Professional workflow require the no aquifer case to be run even when the aquifer is well justified?', '["Because the engine needs the no aquifer oil in place as a starting guess before it can fit an aquifer term", "Because the difference between the two answers measures how much of your result the aquifer is responsible for", "Because a validation tier can only be quoted for a run whose aquifer model was set to none", "Because the drive indices cannot be apportioned unless a case with zero water influx is available for comparison"]'::jsonb, 1, 'One extra run and one extra line turn a claim into a measurement. On Ekene the pair reads 12139208.1074968 stb without the aquifer and -516449.043355256 stb with an unnecessary one, which is a finding rather than an embarrassment.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 13, 'After four steps on Ahmed 10-10 the aquifer has delivered 37973154.4101719 bbl against an encroachable 211934253.721285 bbl. What does that ratio support?', '["That 0.179174219095844 has been drawn, which is consistent with an aquifer still able to support pressure", "That the model has failed, because a real aquifer cannot deliver more than a tenth of its encroachable water", "That the aquifer is nearly exhausted, since almost a fifth of its total capacity has already been produced", "That the encroachment angle was applied twice, since the fraction should sit much nearer to one half"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under a fifth of the account has been spent, and the aquifer pressure has fallen from 2740 psia to 2249.06263967739 psia. A fraction approaching or exceeding one would not describe a strong aquifer, it would describe a wrong one.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 14, 'The engine marches Ahmed 10-10 to 37.9731544101719 MMbbl where the book prints 37.971 MMbbl. What is the value of that comparison?', '["It proves the engine is more accurate than the published table, whose author rounded every intermediate value before printing it", "It shows a table typed from a book and an independent implementation reached the same place, to 0.00567383048077399 percent", "It shows the capstone tolerance of 0.4 MMbbl was set generously, since the two answers agree so closely", "It confirms that the midpoint convention is optional, since either convention lands within the tolerance"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Two independent routes to the same influx, one printed in a textbook and one computed by code you did not write, is what makes the number worth reporting. Agreement to that precision also confirms the conventions in the implementation match the ones the book used.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 15, 'You are working the capstone in the two panels. Which combination of settings produces every graded field correctly?', '["Both panels left at their defaults throughout, since every graded field is read from the configuration as loaded", "Book geometry with the pseudo steady state denominator, and the Ekene selector on none for all six fields", "Plain ln(reD) for the productivity index, with the Ekene selector moved to pot for every field in the form", "Book geometry with the pseudo steady state denominator, and the Ekene selector on pot for the two tank fields"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The four aquifer fields need the published geometry untouched and the pseudo steady state denominator selected. The two tank fields are the only numbers in the course read from a deliberately wrong model, so the aquifer selector has to be moved for them and moved back afterwards.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 1, 'A tank''s ratio of underground withdrawal to total expansion holds the same value at every survey. What does that constancy mean?', '["That the plot of withdrawal against expansion is a straight line running through the origin", "That the surveys were taken at equal pressure intervals, so every expansion term grew in step with the produced volumes recorded beside it", "That the fluid properties were supplied per row rather than interpolated from a table", "That the drive indices at the last survey close to one, whatever aquifer model is applied"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A constant ratio is a straight line through the origin, restated one survey at a time. On the Ekene tank it reads 12139208.1074970 at the first survey and 12139208.1074968 at the sixth, which is what a tank with nothing missing from its model looks like.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 2, 'Why must water influx be modelled rather than measured?', '["Because influx is measured only after abandonment, once the aquifer has finished responding to the depletion imposed on it", "Because the regulator requires an aquifer model to be declared before any influx measurement can be reported", "Because water meters report produced water, not water crossing the original contact", "Because influx can be measured, but only in gas reservoirs, where the p over z line reveals it"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nothing meters water crossing the original contact. The volume that entered has to be inferred from a model of a body of rock nobody has logged, which is why an aquifer term is always a claim rather than a reading.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 3, 'A tank is receiving water and the analyst leaves the aquifer term out of the balance entirely. Which way does the reported oil in place move?', '["It moves down, because the missing water reduces the expansion the oil is asked to supply", "It stays put, because the regression compensates through the intercept rather than the slope", "It moves up, because the extra withdrawal now has to be explained by more oil expanding instead", "It moves in whichever direction the residuals require, so no general statement about the direction is possible"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Water influx and oil expansion are competing explanations for the same withdrawal. Forcing an aquifer onto the Ekene tank pushed the reported oil in place down past zero, and leaving out a real one runs the same mechanism in reverse.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 4, 'The engine reported drive mechanism on the Ekene tank as committed is depletion_drive and its aquifer strength is none. What is the water drive index?', '["Exactly 0.00000000000000, which is what a tank with no influx and no produced water gives", "0.607003891050583, the complement of the rock and connate water share", "Slightly negative, since a small allowance for produced water is always subtracted from the index whatever the case", "0.392996108949419, the same as the rock and connate water share of the expansion"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The index is the influx less the produced water, over the withdrawal. Ekene has neither, so it is exactly zero, and the classification the engine attaches follows from that.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 5, 'How does the engine form the water drive index for an oil case?', '["As the cumulative influx less the produced water in reservoir barrels, over the withdrawal", "As the cumulative influx divided by the total expansion at that timestep", "As one minus the sum of the depletion and rock and connate water indices, which is how closure is guaranteed", "As the cumulative influx divided by the encroachable water of the aquifer supplying it"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The numerator is the water that came in less the water that went back out at reservoir conditions, and the denominator is the gross withdrawal. The water you produced back is netted off before the index is formed.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 6, 'What does adding an aquifer term to a material balance regression do to the fit, regardless of whether the physics needs it?', '["It leaves the fit unchanged until the influx exceeds a threshold fraction of the withdrawal", "It gives the regression a second freedom, which it will spend whether or not the data called for it", "It constrains the regression further, so the fit statistic falls whenever the aquifer term turns out to be unnecessary", "It forces the intercept to zero, which is why an aquifer run cannot be diagnosed from its intercept"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A least squares fit uses whatever freedom it is handed to reduce residuals. That is why a healthy looking fit on an aquifer run is not evidence for the aquifer, and why the diagnostic has to be read before the model is chosen.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 7, 'Two analysts disagree about whether a tank has an aquifer. Which piece of evidence settles it without assuming either answer?', '["The fit statistic of the run that includes an aquifer, compared against the run that excludes one", "The drive mechanism string the engine reports once an aquifer model has been supplied to it", "The validation tier attached to the aquifer path they are proposing to use for the case", "The survey by survey ratio of withdrawal to expansion, computed before any model is chosen"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The ratio column comes from measured pressures, produced volumes and fluid properties alone. Every other candidate already contains one of the two answers, so it cannot arbitrate between them.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 8, 'Which aquifer model does not require the observation dates in the production history?', '["Fetkovich, since its decay term already carries the step length inside the exponential it forms", "The pot aquifer, which responds instantly and has no marching scheme to advance in time", "Carter-Tracy, because it superposes over pressure steps rather than over elapsed days", "None of them, since the engine validates every date before dispatching to a model"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A pot aquifer delivers water as a function of pressure drop alone. The routine that extracts step lengths is called only by the time marching models, and it raises a named error on a missing or non increasing date.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 9, 'Forcing a pot aquifer onto the aquifer free Ekene tank returns an oil in place of -516449.043355256 stb against a true 12139208.1074968 stb. What is the relative error?', '["-4.25438824989180 percent, the fraction of the true value the wrong answer represents", "-95.7456117501082 percent, which is what a slope of that magnitude and sign implies", "It has no meaningful relative error, because a negative oil in place is not a physical quantity at all", "-104.254388249892 percent of the true value, so the answer has moved past zero and out the other side"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The error exceeds one hundred percent because the answer did not merely shrink, it changed sign. The negative reading is the only thing on the results screen that betrays the mistake.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 10, 'In that same wrong model run, what volume of water does the regression attribute to the aquifer?', '["211934253.721285 rb, the encroachable water of the aquifer geometry the engine assumes by default in a pot run", "317926.842484584 rb, matching the underground withdrawal at the final survey exactly", "42890161.1573930 rb, a volume of water larger than every barrel of oil the tank held", "Zero, since the tank has no aquifer and the regression finds no water to attribute"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The regression has to explain the withdrawal with the freedoms it was given, and an aquifer of 42890161.1573930 rb is what closes the books once the oil term has been pushed negative. Imagined water is not a small correction here.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 11, 'The fit statistic on that wrong model run is 0.999485673716372. What is the correct reading of that number?', '["The model reproduces the data closely, so the aquifer term is supported by the survey history", "The fit is degraded relative to the true model, which is how a wrong aquifer is normally caught in practice", "The fit is meaningless because the slope is negative, so the statistic should be discarded", "The points lie close to the fitted relationship, which says nothing about whether it is the right one"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Goodness of fit measures internal consistency between a model and the points it was fitted to. It cannot compare that model against a different one, and on this run it stays high while the answer is physically impossible.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 12, 'The same wrong model run is classified strong_water_drive with an aquifer strength of strong. Why is that classification not a check on the model?', '["Because the classifier only distinguishes gas from oil fluid systems and ignores the influx entirely", "Because it reads the drive indices the model itself produced, so it inherits the model''s error", "Because strength classifications are advisory and are suppressed whenever the oil in place is negative", "Because the classifier requires at least ten surveys before its output can be relied upon"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine classifies from the water drive index it just computed, which on this run is 1.04254388249892 because an imagined aquifer was doing all the work. A label derived from the model cannot testify about the model.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 13, 'Between the honest Ekene reading and the forced pot aquifer reading, how far does the oil in place move?', '["12655657.1508521 stb, which is more than the tank was ever thought to contain", "516449.043355256 stb, the magnitude of the wrong answer taken on its own", "11622759.0641416 stb, the difference between the two magnitudes once the sign has been discarded", "42890161.1573930 stb, the volume the aquifer term absorbed in reservoir barrels"]'::jsonb, 0, 'From 12139208.1074968 stb to -516449.043355256 stb is a swing of 12655657.1508521 stb. Reporting both readings turns that into a measured consequence of a modelling choice instead of a hidden one.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 14, 'What is the strongest argument for running the no aquifer case even on a tank you are confident has an aquifer?', '["It is required before the engine will accept aquifer parameters in the case inputs at all", "It produces a higher validation tier, which reviewers are known to weigh more heavily than an aquifer run", "It quantifies how much of the reported oil in place actually depends on the aquifer you chose", "It removes the need to state the aquifer geometry, since the comparison stands in for it"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The difference between the two answers is the aquifer''s contribution, expressed in barrels. It costs one run, and without it the reader cannot tell whether the aquifer moved the answer by a rounding difference or by the size of the tank.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 15, 'What does the Fetkovich productivity index depend on?', '["Total compressibility, the connected wedge volume and the initial pressure of the aquifer", "The step length, the encroachable water and the drawdown at the start of the first step of the march", "Porosity, the two radii and the conversion factor from cubic feet to reservoir barrels", "Permeability, thickness, the angle fraction, viscosity and the pseudo steady state group"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The index measures how fast the aquifer can move water for each psi of drawdown, so it is built from transmissibility terms and the geometry of the contact. The volume terms belong to the encroachable water, which is a separate constant.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 16, 'On the Ahmed 10-10 geometry the productivity index computes to 116.496154838747 bbl/d/psi against a printed 116.5. What does that agreement establish?', '["That the encroachment angle was applied twice in the published example and only once in the engine", "That the printed value was itself computed from the pseudo steady state form", "That the index is insensitive to the denominator, since both candidate forms land near the same value", "That the engine rounds its own output to match published values wherever a benchmark exists"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Agreement to 0.00330056759944387 percent is far tighter than the gap between the two candidate denominators, which differ by roughly a factor of two in their effect. The match therefore identifies which form the book used.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 17, 'The group J times initial pressure divided by encroachable water works out at 0.00150612493569796 per day on Ahmed 10-10. What is it?', '["The reciprocal time constant of the aquifer''s response, which the decay term exponentiates", "The daily fraction of the aquifer''s water crossing the contact at the initial drawdown", "The daily pressure decline of the aquifer once its pressure has equalised with the reservoir pressure", "The rate at which the aquifer radius advances into the reservoir, expressed per day"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The decay term is one minus an exponential of that group multiplied by the step length, so the group sets how quickly the aquifer''s response dies away. The book prints 0.001506 for it.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 18, 'The book quotes a reduced constant of 32705 bbl/psi for the Ahmed 10-10 aquifer. How is it formed?', '["By dividing the encroachable water by the initial pressure, giving 77348.2677814910 bbl/psi", "By multiplying the productivity index by the step length in days and by the total compressibility of the water", "By dividing the encroachable water by the initial pressure and multiplying by the decay term", "By dividing the connected wedge by the initial pressure and applying the angle once more"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Collecting the constants that multiply the drawdown in every step gives 32710.3987275100 bbl/psi, which agrees with the printed figure to 0.0165073460022428 percent. That single number times the step drawdown is the influx for the step.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 19, 'In the Fetkovich marching scheme, how is the aquifer pressure at the end of a step obtained?', '["From the reservoir pressure at the end of that step, since the two equalise between steps", "From the previous aquifer pressure less the drawdown that drove the step just completed", "From the productivity index multiplied by the influx delivered during the step in question", "From the initial pressure scaled by one minus cumulative influx over encroachable water"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The aquifer is treated as a tank drawn down in proportion to what it has already delivered. On Ahmed 10-10 that leaves it at 2249.06263967739 psia after four steps, from an initial 2740 psia.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 20, 'The published Ahmed 10-10 table shows a cumulative influx of 13.54 MMbbl after two steps. What does the engine march give?', '["9.61500000000000 MMbbl, which is the influx delivered during the second step alone", "13.5503574169008 MMbbl, agreeing with the printed column to the figures the book prints", "19.2502191391991 MMbbl, since the engine drives each step with the end of step reservoir pressure", "7.85049569460241 MMbbl, since the engine restarts the aquifer pressure at each step"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Two steps into the march the engine has accumulated 13.5503574169008 MMbbl. The book prints 13.54, and the step increment of 9.615 it also prints is a different quantity from the running total.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 21, 'For the Ahmed 10-10 aquifer the ratio of aquifer radius to reservoir radius is quoted as 5. What are the two radii?', '["A reservoir radius of 9200 ft and an aquifer radius of 46000 ft, dividing to exactly 5", "9200 ft and 45000 ft, with the ratio rounded to one significant figure for the printed table", "5000 ft and 25000 ft, the values the radius ratio was originally derived from", "46000 ft and 230000 ft, since the ratio is taken between the aquifer and its outer boundary"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The published geometry gives a reservoir radius of 9200 ft and an aquifer radius of 46000 ft. Dividing them returns the quoted ratio exactly, which is a useful check that you have loaded the right case.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 22, 'Two aquifers have the same encroachable water but one has twice the productivity index. How do they differ over a single time step?', '["They deliver the same volume, since the encroachable water sets the total the aquifer can supply", "The larger index delivers less, because it depletes the aquifer pressure faster and cuts the drawdown available to the step", "The larger index delivers a greater share of the available drawdown, through a larger decay term", "The larger index changes only the final aquifer pressure and leaves the influx per step unaffected"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The decay term rises with the index, so more of the drawdown driving the step is converted into influx before the step ends. The encroachable water caps the eventual total, not the pace at which it arrives.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 23, 'What does the quantity encroachable water divided by initial pressure, 77348.2677814910 bbl/psi on Ahmed 10-10, represent?', '["The rate at which the aquifer loses pressure for every barrel of water it delivers to the reservoir", "The influx per psi of drawdown if the aquifer could respond instantly and completely", "The productivity index expressed in cumulative rather than daily terms", "The volume of water in the connected wedge for each psi of the reservoir''s own pressure drop"]'::jsonb, 1, 'It is the pot aquifer response hiding inside the Fetkovich form: the whole account divided by the pressure that fills it. The decay term is what scales it down to the share an aquifer of finite mobility actually delivers in a step of finite length.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 24, 'For a volumetric gas tank, what is the significance of the point where the p over z line reaches zero?', '["It is the cumulative production that would empty the tank, and therefore the gas in place", "It marks the abandonment pressure at which the reservoir can no longer flow to surface facilities", "It is the pressure at which the gas deviation factor reaches unity and the ideal gas law applies", "It is the point at which water influx begins, since a volumetric tank cannot pass it"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Plotting pressure over the deviation factor against cumulative production gives a straight line for a closed gas tank, and its intercept on the production axis is the volume that would take the tank to zero. That is the gas in place, read as a slope problem rather than a volumetric one.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 25, 'A gas tank with water influx is analysed as though it were volumetric. What happens to the gas in place?', '["It is understated, because the imported water occupies pore space the gas would otherwise have filled", "It is unaffected, because influx changes the pressure but not the cumulative production axis", "It is overstated, because influx holds the pressure up and bends the line away from the trend", "It cannot be estimated at all, because the plot ceases to be a function of production"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Water support keeps the pressure higher than depletion alone would leave it, so the plotted points curve upward from the volumetric straight line. Extrapolating that curve as if it were straight pushes the intercept out to more gas than the tank holds.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 26, 'Which solver methods does the engine expose for material balance?', '["Least squares, graphical fit, trial and error, and Monte Carlo sampling of the input terms", "Volumetric, decline curve, material balance and a simulation history match", "Havlena-Odeh, Carter-Tracy, Fetkovich and van Everdingen-Hurst superposition", "Havlena-Odeh, p over z, modified p over z and the pot aquifer plot for gas tanks"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Those four are the solver methods in the engine''s own union type. They are ways of plotting the balance, and they sit alongside the aquifer models rather than replacing them.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 27, 'What does the modified p over z solver add to the plain one?', '["A correction for the water produced from the reservoir alongside the gas over the same period", "A Ramagost and Farshad correction for formation compressibility", "A correction for the deviation factor where the plain method assumes ideal behaviour", "A correction for the encroachment angle of the aquifer supplying the tank"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The plain plot assumes the pore volume is fixed. The modified form carries the rock compressibility correction of Ramagost and Farshad, which matters most in an overpressured tank where compaction is a real part of the drive.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 28, 'The pot aquifer plot the engine offers for gas systems comes from which source?', '["Pletcher SPE 75354, as the equation numbered thirteen in that paper", "Havlena and Odeh''s second paper, offered as the gas equivalent of the straight line method", "Ramagost and Farshad, as the overpressured extension of their own correction", "Fetkovich, as the limiting form of his scheme when the productivity index is large"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine names Pletcher Equation 13 as the pot aquifer plot for gas. The same paper supplies the two cell gas benchmark the gas paths are checked against.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 29, 'The Pletcher gas case the engine is benchmarked on has a true gas in place of 100.8 Bcf and an aquifer of 74.5 million reservoir barrels. What is its initial pressure?', '["2740 psia, the same initial pressure as the Fetkovich worked example uses for its aquifer", "3200 psia, matching the Ekene oil tank used elsewhere in this course", "6411 psia, an overpressured tank at a reservoir temperature of 239 F", "1460 psia, the pressure reached at the end of the published performance history"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The case is an overpressured gas tank at 6411 psia and 239 F with an initial water saturation of 0.15. The high initial pressure is exactly why the formation compressibility correction matters on that fluid system.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 30, 'Three fluid property sources are available for one case. How does the engine resolve which to use?', '["Once for the whole run, from the declared PVT source recorded in the case metadata", "Separately for each property, but once for the whole history, so provenance is fixed per property", "By blending all three in proportion to confidence weights supplied with the case inputs", "Separately for each property and at each pressure, so provenance can differ within a run"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The precedence chain is evaluated at every lookup. One timestep can take the oil factor from a row, the water factor from a table and the deviation factor from a correlation, and the next timestep can differ if its pressure falls outside the table.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 31, 'A laboratory table carries the oil formation volume factor at every pressure but the solution gas ratio at only some. What does the interpolation return for the missing ratio?', '["The value at the nearest row that has one, since the property varies slowly with pressure", "Nothing, whenever either of the bracketing rows lacks the field, so the lookup falls through", "An average of every row that carries the field, weighted by distance from the target pressure", "A value extrapolated from the two rows that do carry it, flagged with a warning in the result"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Interpolation needs a value at both ends of the bracket. A table can therefore serve one property and quietly hand another to a correlation, which is why provenance has to be checked property by property rather than table by table.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 32, 'A case declares its PVT source as a laboratory table but the file contains neither a table nor per-row PVT columns. What does the engine do?', '["It warns explicitly that the declared source is missing and names the ways to fix it", "It falls back to correlations silently, since the declared source is metadata rather than an instruction to the solver", "It refuses the run, since a declared source that is absent counts as an input error", "It substitutes correlation defaults and marks the validation tier down by one level"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine checks the declaration against what is actually present and says so when the two disagree, listing the options: upload a table, supply per-row columns, or change the declared source. It would rather complain than fall back without telling you.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 33, 'At the last Ekene survey the per-row oil formation volume factor is 1.21589748101760 rb/stb and the correlation path gives 1.25041043403039. What is the difference?', '["0.0175861539277053 percent, small enough that either value serves for engineering work", "9.53012631545501 percent, which is why the reported oil in place moves by that amount", "2.83847557475905 percent, and it is a bias rather than scatter, so it does not average out", "It cannot be compared, since the two factors are quoted at different reference pressures entirely"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The correlation is high by 2.83847557475905 percent at that survey and high at every other survey too. A systematic offset in a fluid property walks into every term built from it and stays there.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 34, 'The engine reports that reservoir conditions sit outside a correlation''s documented training range. What does it do next?', '["It stops the run, since a result outside a correlation''s range cannot be defended to a reviewer", "It switches to the next correlation in the roster whose range does contain the conditions", "It widens the range automatically and records the widening in the validation reference string", "It continues to compute and asks you to read the result as an extrapolation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The warning names the parameters that fall outside and states plainly that the engine continues. That is the right posture: it neither refuses a calculation you may still need nor pretends the extrapolation did not happen.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 35, 'Which set names correlations the engine can use for the bubble point, solution gas ratio and oil formation volume factor?', '["McCain for water, Beggs-Robinson for oil viscosity and Lee-Gonzalez-Eakin for gas viscosity", "Standing, Vasquez-Beggs and Glaso", "Hall-Yarborough, Dranchuk-Abou-Kassem and Sutton", "Havlena-Odeh, Ramagost-Farshad and Pletcher"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Those three are the choices for the oil property group. The others in the engine''s roster cover the water factor, the viscosities and the gas deviation factor, and the last group are not correlations at all but material balance methods.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 36, 'Why does provenance have to be established before the terms are computed rather than afterwards?', '["Because it is the phase where the question is cheap, not an interrogation of an answer", "Because the engine cannot report provenance once a regression has been run over the timesteps", "Because the precedence chain can only be inspected before the first timestep is dispatched to the solver", "Because a validation tier is assigned when the first term is computed and cannot be revised"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Asking where a number came from before you have an answer costs a few minutes. Asking after you have written the oil in place down means arguing with a conclusion you are already attached to, which is a different and much harder task.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 37, 'An aquifer model is chosen because it produced the highest fit statistic among those tried. What is wrong with that?', '["Nothing, provided every model tried used the same convention for the drawdown in each step", "The chosen model will always be the one with the fewest parameters, which biases the choice against real aquifers", "Fit measures consistency with the points, not whether the chosen model suits this reservoir", "Fit statistics cannot be compared across models, since each uses a different regression variable"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Models with more freedom fit better whether or not they are right, and Ekene shows a physically impossible answer sitting behind a fit of 0.999485673716372. The reason to prefer a model has to come from what you know about the aquifer, not from the residuals.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 38, 'Besides the oil in place and the fit, what should be read from an aquifer run before it is reported?', '["The engine version, the solver method and the number of production rows the run accepted", "The intercept, the number of surveys and the units in which the withdrawal was expressed", "The validation tier, the tolerance percentage and the reference string attached to that path", "The drive indices, influx against encroachable water, and where the aquifer pressure ended"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Those three say whether the mechanisms apportion sensibly, whether the aquifer has been asked for more water than it holds, and whether its pressure remains physical. An aquifer that has delivered more than its account is a message rather than a result.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 39, 'Why is writing the diagnosis down, dated, before any model is fitted more than a formality?', '["It is required before the validation tier may be quoted in a reserves memo to a client", "It commits you before the fitting starts, so the diagnosis cannot be quietly revised", "It allows the engine to check your diagnosis against the drive mechanism it classifies", "It shortens the run time, because the aquifer model can be set before the first pass"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nobody intends to rewrite their evidence, and almost everybody drifts once a tidy looking model is on the screen. A written and dated verdict makes the drift visible to you, which is the only reader who can act on it.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 40, 'A memo says only that a Fetkovich aquifer was used. What can a reviewer do with that?', '["Argue with the engineer, but not with the geology, since no input is visible", "Reproduce the influx, since naming the model determines every constant it uses uniquely", "Check the drive index closure, which is enough to confirm the constants were consistent", "Nothing at all, since aquifer models cannot be reviewed without access to the software itself"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A model name without the thickness, porosity, compressibility, permeability, viscosity, radii and encroachment angle leaves nothing to disagree with except the author. Listing the inputs with their sources is what turns a review into a technical conversation.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 41, 'A colleague reports an aquifer case only, with a negative oil in place, and says the fit was excellent. What is the right correction to their reading?', '["Re-run with a larger aquifer, since a negative oil in place indicates the influx was undersized", "Report the magnitude without the sign, since oil in place is conventionally quoted as a positive volume", "Add the run without the aquifer and the diagnostic that motivated it, and let the pair speak", "Raise the tolerance on the oil in place field until the negative result falls inside it"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The negative answer is not a number to be repaired, it is evidence that a term was added which the data did not support. Reporting both runs alongside the ratio column converts an embarrassment into a defensible statement about what the aquifer was worth.'),
  ('mbal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 42, 'At the Professional tier, what does the closing step of stating what would change the answer add over the Associate version?', '["The number of surveys and the total depletion behind the fit, which the Associate version left out", "The validation tier and its tolerance, which become quotable once an aquifer model is present", "The reconciliation against volumetrics, which is only meaningful once influx has been modelled", "The aquifer parameters chosen rather than measured, and the precedence level of each property"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Those two are the exposures this tier created. An aquifer constant that was picked rather than measured, and a fluid property that came from a correlation rather than a laboratory, both belong in the same sentence as the oil in place.');
